#!/usr/bin/env python3
# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-

"""
webdrop [OPTIONS...] [FILE...]

Serve a phone-friendly web page on the LAN for moving files between a mobile
device and this machine.

Uploads land in a per-run directory under ~/Downloads (or under the current
directory when that doesn't exist), created when the first file arrives. Any
FILE given on the command line is offered to the browser for download;
directories are expanded recursively.

    webdrop                       # receive documents, photos, anything
    webdrop report.pdf notes.txt  # also hand those two files to the phone
    webdrop -t                    # require a random token in the URL

The LAN URL is printed at startup, and drawn as a QR code when qrencode is
installed — scanning the terminal beats typing an IP on a phone keyboard.

Traffic is plain HTTP and, without -t, unauthenticated: everyone on the network
can read the offered files and write into the save directory. That is the right
trade at home and the wrong one on shared wifi, where -t makes the URL
unguessable and the QR code keeps it free to enter.

Python 3 standard library only.
"""

